.TH GIRLREPO 1 2026-10-11 girlpages "girlpages Manual"
.SH NAME
girlrepo \- build and publish girlpages repositories and their website
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-C
.IR file ]
.B ingest
.RI [ options ]
.IR path ...
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B ingest \-\-system
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B girlify
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ name ...]
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.B publish
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B girlrepo
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.BR gc " | " search " | " serve " | " status
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B girlrepo
collects man pages from packages and systems, girlifies them, and publishes
them as a
.I repository
\(em a tree of JSON files any static web host can serve \(em with a website on
top.
.BR girl (1)
can fetch pages from a repository instead of asking a model, and the website
lets anyone browse and search them, with a plain HTML page for every man page
so search engines can find them.
.PP
.I dir
(the current directory unless
.B \-d
is given) is the website; the repository lives in
.IR dir /repo .
Upload the whole of
.I dir
to publish both.
Every command that changes the repository takes a lock on it, so they can be
run from cron or CI without stepping on each other.
.PP
The usual cycle is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
girlrepo ingest \-\-distro debian \-\-release trixie *.deb
girlrepo girlify
girlrepo publish \-\-url https://example.org \-\-gc
.fi
.RE
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "ingest \fIpath\fR..."
Adds every English man page found in each
.IR path ,
which may be a directory (a manual root such as
.BR /usr/share/man ,
or any tree containing one, like an unpacked package or a whole system
image) or a package file:
.BR .deb ,
.BR .pkg.tar.* ,
or
.B .rpm
(source packages are skipped).
Packages are unpacked with
.BR tar (1)
and
.BR cpio (1);
neither dpkg nor rpm is needed.
Translations
.RI ( man/de/man1 ,
and so on) are left out.
.PP
Each page is identified by the SHA-256 of its roff source, decompressed, with
.B .so
requests replaced by what they include, and symlinks followed (inside the
unpacked package, for absolute links).
The same page from different packages or distributions is stored once, noting
everywhere it was found.
A page whose source changed becomes a new version, and the current one unless
.B \-\-keep\-current
is given; old versions are kept.
When one run finds different versions of the same page (the same name and
section), the one from the last
.I path
given is current, so list older sources first.
New versions are rendered with
.BR man (1)
to make their
.B man
variant.
If
.BR girl (1)'s
cache already has a conversion of the same text, that becomes the
.B girl
variant straight away.
.TP
.B \-\-system
Ingest this system's manpath instead of paths, noting which installed package
(from the dpkg, pacman or rpm database) each page came from, and the
distribution from
.BR os\-release (5).
.TP
.BR \-\-distro =\fIid\fR ", " \-\-release =\fIrelease\fR
Record where the pages came from, such as
.B debian
and
.BR trixie .
.TP
.BR \-\-package =\fIname\fR ", " \-\-package\-version =\fIversion\fR ", " \-\-arch =\fIarch\fR
Record the package, for directories, or for a single package file whose name
doesn't follow its format's convention.
Package files are otherwise identified by their names.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-current
Don't make the ingested versions current: for adding older releases after
newer ones.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sections =\fIlist\fR
Only these sections.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs =\fIn\fR
Render this many pages at once (default: one per CPU).
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Say what would be added.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-cache
Don't take conversions from girl's cache.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Only report problems.
.SS "girlify [\fIname\fR...]"
Converts the current version of every page (or just the named ones) that has
no
.B girl
variant yet, using the backend and model from girl's configuration (see
.BR girl (1)).
Aliases sharing a source are converted once.
Interrupting it is safe: each page is saved as it's done, and the next run
carries on.
.TP
.BR \-\-backend =\fIname\fR ", " \-\-model =\fImodel\fR
Override the configured backend and model.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-jobs =\fIn\fR
Convert this many pages at once (default from the configuration).
.TP
.BR \-\-limit =\fIn\fR
Stop after this many pages.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sections =\fIlist\fR
Only these sections.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-versions
Convert old versions too.
.TP
.BR \-\-batch =\fIapi\fR ", " \-\-no\-batch
Send pages in batches with
.BR openrouter " or " anthropic ,
or not at all, overriding the
.B batch
setting.
Batches work as for
.BR "girl \-\-bulk" ;
see
.BR girl (1).
What was sent is saved in
.BR ~/.cache/girlpages/girlrepo\-batches/ ,
and the next
.B girlify
collects whatever has finished.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-wait
Send batches off and return without waiting.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Convert again, even pages that already have a
.B girl
variant.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run ", " \-\-no\-cache ", " \-q ", " \-\-quiet
As for
.BR ingest .
.SS publish
Writes the section indexes, the search index and
.BR manifest.json ,
then the website.
Until it runs, nothing ingested or girlified since the last publish is
visible.
Website files are only rewritten when they change, so syncing the result
somewhere is cheap.
.TP
.BR \-\-url =\fIurl\fR
The site's absolute URL, for canonical links and
.BR sitemap.xml .
.TP
.BR \-\-base =\fIpath\fR
The URL path the site is served from, if not
.BR / ,
such as
.B /girlpages/
for a GitHub Pages project site.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-site
Only update the repository.
.TP
.B \-\-gc
Collect garbage afterwards.
.PP
The website is a progressive web app: it can be installed, and a service
worker keeps the pages someone has read (up to 300) and the app itself for
offline use.
Repository files fetched by the browser are kept in
.BR localStorage :
content-addressed ones forever, until space runs out (the least recently used
go first), and
.B manifest.json
as a fallback for when there's no network.
Every half hour while a page is open, the browser looks at
.BR manifest.json ;
when it has changed, the shards the new one doesn't use are deleted from
localStorage in idle time.
.SS gc
Deletes files nothing refers to any more: superseded listings, section indexes
and index shards, and the contents of versions no listing mentions.
Content-addressed files may be cached by browsers and proxies forever, so it is
kinder to collect garbage some time after publishing rather than straight
away.
.B \-n
says what would go.
.SS "search \fIquery\fR..."
Searches the published index the way the website does.
.B \-\-repo
searches a repository elsewhere;
.B \-l
sets how many results.
.SS serve
Serves
.I dir
at
.B http://localhost:1990/
(or
.BR \-\-listen )
to preview the site.
.SS status
Counts pages, versions and conversions.
.SH "REPOSITORY FORMAT"
Everything is JSON.
Files named
.IB name \- sha .json
or
.IB sha .json
are named after the SHA-256 of their own contents and never change, so they
can be cached forever.
.B manifest.json
and the pointer files change in place and should be cached briefly.
.TP
.B manifest.json
The root: sections, page counts and the index manifests.
.TP
.BI contents/ ab / source / variant .json
One version of a page.
.TP
.BI page/name\- section / name .json
Points at the page's listing.
.TP
.BI page/page\- ab / sha .json
Every version of a page, where each was found, and its variants' hashes.
.TP
.BI section/ section .json
Points at the section's index.
.TP
.BI section/ section \- sha .json
Every page in a section, with its description and listing hash.
.TP
.BI index/words/manifest\- sha .json
The range of words in each word shard.
.TP
.BI index/words/shard\- ab / sha .json
Words, each with the pages it appears in as [doc id, score].
.TP
.BI index/grams/manifest\- sha .json
The range of trigrams in each gram shard.
.TP
.BI index/grams/shard\- ab / sha .json
Trigrams, each with the words containing it, for finding misspelt words.
.TP
.BI index/docs/manifest\- sha .json
The shards of the page table that doc ids index.
.TP
.BI index/docs/shard\- ab / sha .json
Part of the page table: [name, section, description].
.PP
.I source
is the page's source hash (as above) and
.I ab
its first two characters.
Variants are
.B girl
(girlpages markup, split into sections at top-level headings) and
.B man
(man's plain text, split at its headings, with the header and footer lines
apart); joining a variant's sections gives back exactly the original.
Names and sections are escaped in file names: anything but letters, digits
and
.B _+\-.,:@
(or a leading dot) becomes
.BI % XX\fR,
which must be percent-encoded again in URLs.
.PP
A client with a page installed needs one request:
.IB repo /contents/ ab / source /girl.json\fR.
The website's search reads the manifests, then only the shards a query needs.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR dir /repo/
The repository.
.TP
.IR dir /repo/.lock
Held while the repository is being changed.
.TP
.IR dir /man/ section / name /
A page on the website; old versions are under
.BI v/ source /\fR,
and girlified pages have the original under
.BR original/ .
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on success, 1 for a usage error, 2 if something failed, 130 if interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B girlrepo ingest \-\-system && girlrepo girlify \-\-limit 100 && girlrepo publish
Start a repository from this machine, girlifying the first hundred pages.
.TP
.B girlrepo ingest \-\-distro arch \-\-release rolling /var/cache/pacman/pkg/*.pkg.tar.zst
Add every page from Arch's package cache.
.TP
.B girlrepo publish \-\-base /girlpages/ \-\-url https://naomiamethyst.github.io/girlpages
Build for a GitHub Pages project site.
.TP
.B girl \-\-repo ./repo ls
Read
.BR ls (1)
from a repository on disk.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR girl (1),
.BR man (1)
